  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 344 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nA strong upper ridge remains over much of the Plains with the ridge \naxis extending down from the Northern Plains to the Central Plains. \nIn our area, dewpoints range from the upper 60s in central KS to the \nupper 70s in southeast KS. This moisture combined with high \ntemperatures near 100 has resulted in heat indices greater than 105 \nacross the area. The warmest heat indices reside across south-\ncentral, southeast and portions of central KS where values are \ngreater than 110. As such, the Extreme Heat Warning has been \nexpanded north and remains in effect through 8 PM this evening. As \nwe move into tonight and Sunday morning, low temperatures will \nremain 5-10 degrees above normal with lows in the upper 70s. \n\nThe upper-level pattern for our area is progged to remain the same \nfor Sunday through Monday with an upper high remaining over the \ncentral/southern Rockies and into the central/southern Plains. Above \nnormal 850 mb temperatures are forecast with the ECMWF showing an \n850 mb temperature anomaly between 4-8 C and the GFS between 7-12 C. \nThis will result in high temperatures between 100-105 with heat \nindices between 105-110 each afternoon into the evening. For Sunday, \nthe warmest heat indices are anticipated in far southeast KS where \ndewpoints in the low 70s are expected, resulting in heat indices \nexceeding 110 degrees. As such, an Extreme Heat Warning is now in \neffect for southeast KS from noon to 8 PM on Sunday. For Monday, \nhigh temperatures near 105 are likely for central and south-central \nKS with highs near 100 in southeast KS. Even though hotter high \ntemperatures are forecast for central and south-central KS, lower \ndewpoints are progged with values in the low 60s. This will keep \nheat indices greater than 105 across the area, but likely less than \n110 degrees. A Heat Advisory remains in effect across the area \nthrough 8 PM Tuesday. \n\nThe upper ridge will break down Monday night into Tuesday, which \nwill limit the warmest conditions to southern KS on Tuesday \nafternoon. High temperatures will range from the mid 90s in central \nKS to near 100 in southern KS. This will result in the warmest heat \nindices in southern KS with values around 105 degrees. The ridge \naxis is progged to shift westward towards the Central Great Basin \nand Desert Southwest late in the week, though high temperatures will \ngenerally remain above normal with highs in the 90s forecast through \nthe end of next week with low temperatures in the 70s. For \nprecipitation chances, as the upper ridge breaks down, this will \ngive way to low chances for showers and storms starting Monday \nnight. These chances will be possible each night through the end of \nthe week. At this point, the best chance for rainfall looks to be \nThursday night into Friday as guidance suggests a more pronounced \nshortwave trough to slide across the region. Stay tuned for forecast \nupdates.",
